PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
  "current_consensus": 40,
  "vote_breakdown": {
    "40": ["Colleague 1", "Colleague 2 (switched)", "Colleague 3", "Colleague 4", "Colleague 5", "Colleague 6", "Colleague 8", "Colleague 9", "Colleague 10", "Colleague 11", "Colleague 12", "Colleague 15"],
    "60": ["Colleague 7", "Colleague 13", "Colleague 14"]
  },
  "key_arguments_and_evidence": {
    "lower_bound_tightness": "The profile with n = 3t = 60 agents, a symmetric Condorcet 3-cycle on {1, 2, 3} (20 agents each), and item 4 unanimously ranked last is undisputed across all 15 colleagues. It rigorously proves that u >= 2t = 40 is strictly necessary (all multi-item sets fail Condition 1, empty set requires 60, singletons face outside load 40).",
    "backward_elimination_invariant": "Backward greedy elimination terminates at some set O. If |O| >= 2, at most 4 - |O| <= 2 items are eliminated. Since each eliminated item transfers at most t votes at its elimination step, the maximum load an external item can have against O is at most (4 - |O|)t <= 2t = 40! Thus, backward elimination directly guarantees u <= 40 unless it collapses to a singleton |O| = 1.",
    "resolution_of_singleton_collapse": "If backward elimination collapses sequentially (x1 -> x2 -> x3 -> {x4}): x3 has load <= t = 20 against x4; x2 has load <= 2t = 40 against x4. The only way {x4} fails Condition (2) at u = 40 is if x1 has N(x1 > x4) > 40. Then consider the pair {x1, x4}: if N(x4 > x1) > t, {x1, x4} satisfies Condition (1), and its outside loads from x2 and x3 are each <= 2t = 40, making {x1, x4} suitable! If N(x4 > x1) <= t, pairwise margins force heavy edges >= n - t, which implies n <= 3t = 60 by cycle transitivity, capping all outside loads at n - t <= 2t = 40.",
    "refutation_of_the_fourth_candidate_objection": "Colleagues 7, 13, and 14 argue that a fourth candidate w could cycle (w > z > {x, y} > w). But Colleague 11's backward elimination structural decomposition completely sidesteps this cyclic dependency: backward elimination itself already guarantees u <= 40 for any profile where a multi-item set survives, and when it reaches a singleton, the dominant external item x1 forms a valid pair or limits n <= 60."
  },
  "status_and_strategy": "The proof for u = 40 is now completely closed and mathematically airtight from multiple independent angles (tournament transitivity, set intersection property, and backward elimination set-size invariant). Colleague 2 already switched to 40. In subsequent rounds, emphasize Colleague 11's set-size invariant on backward elimination to bring Colleagues 7, 13, and 14 over to 40."
}

