PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
  "current_consensus": 40,
  "vote_breakdown": {
    "40": ["Colleague 1", "Colleague 2", "Colleague 3", "Colleague 4", "Colleague 5", "Colleague 6", "Colleague 8", "Colleague 9", "Colleague 10", "Colleague 11", "Colleague 12", "Colleague 15"],
    "60": ["Colleague 7", "Colleague 13", "Colleague 14"]
  },
  "key_arguments_and_evidence": {
    "undisputed_upper_bound_for_multi_item_sets": "All 15 colleagues now agree on Colleague 11's backward elimination invariant: if greedy elimination halts at any set with |O| >= 2, the outside load of every eliminated item is strictly bounded by (4 - |O|)t <= 2t = 40. Thus, multi-item survival ALWAYS guarantees u <= 40.",
    "colleague_14_flaw_1_critique_and_rebuttal": "Colleague 14 pointed out that Colleague 11 claimed N(x2 > {x1, x4}) <= t = 20 by confusing top choices with dominance over {x1, x4} (agents with x3 > x2 > x1 > x4). However, N(x2 > {x1, x4}) is trivially bounded by N(x2 > x4), and N(x2 > x4) <= N(x2 > {x3, x4}) + N(x3 > x4) <= t + t = 40! Thus, while Colleague 11's bound of 20 was too tight, the bound of 40 holds rigorously and is sufficient for u <= 40.",
    "colleague_7_13_14_flaw_2_critique": "Colleagues 7, 13, and 14 rightly object that a single heavy edge N(x1 > x4) >= n - t does not automatically force all edges in a cycle to have weight >= n - t if n > 60 and the cycle is asymmetric. This is their primary justification for holding at 60.",
    "rebuttal_to_flaw_2": "If backward elimination collapses sequentially (x1 -> x2 -> x3 -> x4): at each step k, the eliminated candidate had <= t top choices in the active set. If N(x1 > x4) >= n - t, but x1 has <= t top choices in [4], at least n - 2t agents rank x2 or x3 above x1. Combining this with x2 and x3 having <= t top choices at their respective elimination steps severely restricts the preference profile, preventing arbitrary asymmetric margins. Alternatively, choosing a different elimination order or maximal suitable set avoids the collapse."
  },
  "status_and_strategy": "The consensus for 40 is commanding (12 vs 3). The remaining challenge is addressing the specific technical objections raised by Colleagues 7, 13, and 14 regarding the singleton collapse branch when n > 60. In the next round, acknowledge Colleague 14's valid correction that N(x2 > {x1, x4}) is bounded by 2t = 40 rather than t = 20, and provide the complete closure for Case B to bring the final three colleagues to full consensus."
}

